# Cursor rules for this repo — see also CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, and .cursor/rules/architecture.mdc (full detail)

Before any change: read docs/FUNCTIONALITY.md (what the product does) and docs/ARCHITECTURE.md (how it's built). Update whichever doc your change affects, in the same commit — not a follow-up.

Core rules:
1. Reuse existing utilities (e.g. models.FormatMoney, slugify(), resolvePromoCode()) — search before writing a near-duplicate. A duplicate money-formatting function once silently disagreed with the real one; that's the bug class this rule prevents.
2. New features follow the layered pattern additively: models -> repo (all SQL) -> handlers -> templates -> routes.go. Reference: core/repo/audit_requests.go + consultancy.go's Audit checkout — a 4th paid product added without touching existing checkout code.
3. Design for horizontal scale: stateless serverless function, no in-process state across requests except the pooled DB connection. Run independent DB lookups on hot paths concurrently.
4. Every migration must be idempotent — this caused a real outage. Bootstrap() treats any migration error as fatal for the WHOLE app on EVERY cold start, not just the feature. CREATE ... IF NOT EXISTS / ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS are safe as-is. ADD CONSTRAINT has no IF NOT EXISTS form in Postgres — wrap it in `DO $$ BEGIN IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_constraint WHERE conname = '...') THEN ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT ...; END IF; END $$;`. Applying a migration manually to unblock work does NOT make the checked-in file safe — it still runs for real on the next cold start.
5. Never commit secrets. .env/.env.local stay gitignored; .env.example holds placeholders only. Check the diff before pushing anything config-adjacent. Third-party keys — even ones that end up public in page HTML, like the JDoodle embed keys — live only in Vercel environment variables. Never write their values into code, docs, commit messages, or Mem0.
6. Payment/access confirmation is server-side (HMAC-verified) only — never trust a client-reported success.
7. Stay consistent with the stack: Go stdlib net/http, server-rendered html/template, plain CSS (existing design tokens), raw SQL via database/sql, rem units. No framework additions for a single feature.

8. Keep project memory current — write it down as you go. Agents here share no conversation history, so record in Mem0 (via the Mem0 MCP server), as you learn it: decisions and their rationale, traps discovered the hard way, corrections to earlier assumptions (keep both the wrong answer and the right one), and requirement changes including what was ruled out and why. Several specific memories beat one long one — they surface independently on search. Batching it to the end of a session is how it gets lost.

9. The repo is CODE ONLY — no content data, no profile-row exception. Migrations carry schema only (CREATE/ALTER/index/constraint/function): no content INSERT and no content UPDATE, for any table, including the profile identity row. UI chrome (labels, headings, nav, button copy) stays in code. Content lives in the live database; an idempotent snapshot portfolio_seed_data.sql is kept in a Google Drive folder named Portfolio. Refer to that folder by name only — never a URL. Direction of truth: edit in the admin console, then regenerate the snapshot from the live database (core/db/gen_bootstrap_data.sql). The snapshot is a bootstrap copy, not an authoring surface. A new feature ships DDL only; rows are added through admin, then the snapshot is regenerated. The repo alone cannot rebuild content — disaster recovery depends on the snapshot.

10. Docker Compose is the local stack, and it stays paired with .env.example. `docker compose up --build` is the same on Docker Desktop for Windows and for Mac. It starts Postgres (service db, user/password/database portfolio) and the app. .env.example DATABASE_URL is postgres://portfolio:portfolio@db:5432/portfolio?sslmode=disable. Host db and those credentials match docker-compose.yml. Compose mounts LOCAL_CONTENT_DIR (default ../portfolio-local-content, forward slashes) at /content. docker/local-admin.sql is the placeholder admin and is not a migration. .env.example sets GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URL=http://localhost:8080/login/google/callback and SITE_URL=http://localhost:8080. A change to any of these updates .env.example, README.md, docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, and Mem0 in the same piece of work.

11. Course and article content goes through one pipeline. Every lesson/article upload passes storage.NormalizeLesson (site tokens, no in-lesson TOC, unnumbered <h2>, labelled code blocks; <!-- vb:theme dark --> for dark app-style lessons); offline edits use go run ./cmd/lessonfmt. Verify at 390 px and desktop in light and dark, keep the originals as a rollback copy (Storage backup/{date}/ in the same bucket, or the Drive folder Portfolio), then clear courses.outline and flag the chatbot reindex. Full strategy and the owner's design references (UI UX Pro Max, Emil Kowalski, Watermelon UI, Brand-building skills) are in docs/DESIGN.md — read it before any redesign or content update.

12. Product rules that must not regress. Standing product rules (Oct 2026). Auth is stateless signed cookies (password + Google, security-question reset, `token_version` revocation, DB-backed throttling in `auth_throttle`); admin is a single bootstrap admin re-verified against the `admins` table. Course checkout is courses-only, fixed server-side price, email-only for guests (claim code or Google-verified email attaches a purchase), promo codes resolved by `resolvePromoCode`, amount never taken from the client, an optional contribution after a course verified by signature. Software stays login-only and issues Ed25519 licences (`core/licence`; private key only in the host env, never in the repo). Quiz: 5 non-overlapping sets per course bank, a hard 5-attempt cap enforced by a DB trigger, only an admin grants extras, correct answers never sent before submit; certificates are plain ASCII with a public verify page. Booking is public, no login, hourly 10:00-20:00 IST, no overlaps enforced by the DB, honeypot + throttle, private manage link. SEO titles/descriptions/keywords live in the database (`page_seo` and per-item columns, admin /admin/seo) with template fallbacks; renaming a course slug leaves a 301 in `slug_redirects`. The in-lesson compiler uses embed keys held only in the host env (`JDOODLE_EMBED_KEYS`); keys never appear in the repo, docs, page HTML or memory files. No course-specific content, prices, keys or personal data belong in this repo or its docs. Transactional email is sent in the same request, for every completed order, including a zero amount and a promo, every booking, and every consultancy connect request. Admin resend and a once-daily Vercel cron are the only retries. There is no GitHub email worker and no detached goroutine. Resend sends course, software, article, and contribution mail. Brevo sends bookings, connect requests, and the newsletter confirmation. Admin BCC on every message. Keys stay in the host env. No marketing email without recorded consent. Password reset stays the security question.

13. CI minutes are free here, so Actions are not restricted. This repo is public, so GitHub Actions minutes are free and unlimited: workflows may run on any branch, PR or schedule. (GitHub itself only runs schedule triggers from the default branch, so the reindex poller runs from main regardless.) Deploys are Vercel's own Git integration, not Actions. If this repo ever becomes private, restore a main-only guard (if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main' on each job) and a minutes budget, as the private Temple repo has.

Full detail lives in CLAUDE.md and .cursor/rules/architecture.mdc — read those for the complete rules and rationale.

Also read `docs/DESIGN.md` (design tokens, motion rules, components) before touching anything visual.

### 14. Every visitor-facing text field uses `checkField` — no hand-rolled checks
Any field a visitor (not the admin) can type into must be declared with `fieldSpec`/`checkField` (`core/handlers/formcheck.go`) and its kind: `fieldName` (one to three words of English letters), `fieldLine` / `fieldText` (English only, length-capped), `fieldPhone`, `fieldEmail` (ASCII). Use `firstFieldError(spec, value, ...)` to check several at once, put a matching `maxlength` on the input, and add the field to the table in `docs/FUNCTIONALITY.md` (Form field rules). Public forms also get a honeypot and a per-IP `a.throttled`/`a.recordFailure` limit. New kinds or tighter rules go into `checkField`, never a second copy in a handler. Admin-only forms and Google-supplied names are exempt.

### 15. No inline script handlers; every inline script carries the nonce

The page policy has no `'unsafe-inline'` for scripts. Never write `onclick=`/`onsubmit=` or any `on*=` attribute: use a `data-` attribute and handle it in `web/static/js/actions.js` (existing: `data-confirm`, `data-select-on-click`, `data-autosubmit`, `data-select-all`). A template `<script>` block needs `nonce="{{$.Nonce}}"` (HTML built in Go goes through `withScriptNonce`). Scripts from other origins need that origin in `core/middleware/security.go` first. `view.TestTemplatesRespectScriptPolicy` enforces this. Public forms that send mail or create records also call `a.humanVerified(r)` (Cloudflare Turnstile) once the field checks pass.
